      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Understanding that different layers of network protocols can sometimes miss certain types of data doesn't immediately imply that these observations would equate to privacy in a general sense. Non-standard protocols or encryption headers can indeed make your digital footprint more obscure, but this doesn't inherently grant you jurisdiction beyond legal or agreed-upon standards. If you wish to \"reinvent\" such practices, it's essential to recognize that doing so still falls within the larger context of cybersecurity and network health. Collaborations, whether within a community or formally, would be necessary to ensure a responsible approach. Mechanisms like transferring the current \"non-standard\" approach to formal adherence could help protect and legitimize your methods while also, following contributions against national or organizational frameworks. Balancing innovation with shared structures might not be an ideal, but being prepared to regularize and authenticate non-standard approaches can pave the way for new, widely accepted solutions.",
